(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-arterial-occlusive-aneurysm-vignettes"]={"title":"Arterial Occlusive Disease and Aortic Aneurysm Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 69-year-old man has sudden severe pain in his right leg. Pulses are absent below the knee. Which examination finding is often the earliest neurologic sign of acute vascular insufficiency?","choices":["Sensory loss over the foot's dorsum","Complete paralysis of the leg","Absent patellar reflex","Loss of hair on the shin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Light-touch sensory fibers are highly susceptible to ischemia, so sensory deficits over the dorsum of the foot are often the earliest neurologic sign of acute vascular insufficiency. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has a 3.1 cm femoral pseudoaneurysm that has persisted since his catheterization. Which treatment seals the leak?","choices":["Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Ultrasound-guided thrombin injection","Watch and wait","Intravenous heparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of a pseudoaneurysm that does not regress is ultrasound-guided injection of thrombin, which promotes clotting and seals the leak. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with sudden leg ischemia has sensory loss extending beyond the toes with rest pain, mild-to-moderate weakness, an inaudible arterial Doppler signal and an audible venous signal. Where should his evaluation and treatment take place?","choices":["Nowhere; plan major amputation","In the surgical suite, without delay","After outpatient duplex imaging","After computed tomography first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An immediately threatened limb is salvageable only with immediate revascularization, so it should preferentially undergo further evaluation and treatment in a surgical suite. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with hypertension and an acute dissection has had his systolic pressure lowered to 110 mmHg and his pain controlled. Which definitive treatment removes the diseased segment?","choices":["Surgical grafting of the aorta","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Ultrasound-guided thrombin","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beyond blood pressure reduction and pain management, surgery grafts and replaces the diseased portion of the aorta in aortic dissection. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a 4.8 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm has a history of severe reaction to contrast dye. She is stable, and the extent of the aneurysm must be defined. Which imaging is most appropriate?","choices":["Conventional angiography","Magnetic resonance imaging","Computed tomography angiography","Abdominal X-ray"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Magnetic resonance imaging is comparable to computed tomography without exposure to dye or ionizing radiation, which makes it useful in stable patients with a dye allergy. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has an incidental 4.0 cm ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm on a chest radiograph and follow-up imaging. He has no symptoms. Which surveillance plan is appropriate?","choices":["Abdominal ultrasound every three years with no other imaging","Open repair with aortic root replacement within the month","Computed tomography or magnetic resonance every 3 to 5 years","Computed tomography or magnetic resonance every 6 to 12 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic aneurysms under 4.5 cm are followed with computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging every three to five years; 4.5 to 5.4 cm moves to every six to 12 months. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension reports calf pain after walking about two blocks that resolves with rest. His ankle-brachial index is 0.62 on the left and normal on the right. Which interpretation fits the left-sided value?","choices":["Rest pain and tissue loss range","Noncompressible artery range","Claudication range","Normal range"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An ankle-brachial index between 0.4 and 0.9 is the claudication band, matching exertional calf pain relieved by rest. The lower of the two legs is taken as the overall index. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has had a cold, pulseless right leg for three hours, with pallor, tingling and mild weakness of the toes. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Immediate vascular surgery consult","Outpatient ankle-brachial index","Supervised exercise referral","Start cilostazol and follow up"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute limb ischemia is a vascular emergency; management begins with an immediate vascular surgery consult, anticoagulation with intravenous heparin, and testing chosen by the category of ischemia. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man who smokes a pack a day has calf claudication and an ankle-brachial index of 0.7. He asks what matters most for his legs. Which counseling point is most important?","choices":["Stop smoking completely","Limit walking to avoid pain","Elevate the legs when painful","Switch to occasional cigars"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cigarette smoking triples the risk of peripheral artery disease compared with nonsmokers, and smoking cessation heads the list of risk-factor reduction measures for every patient with the disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has pain in both legs when walking, with numbness and weakness. Standing still does not help; the pain eases only when he sits down or leans forward over a shopping cart. His pedal pulses are normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Spinal stenosis","Peripheral artery disease","Peripheral neuropathy","Venous insufficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Spinal stenosis gives claudication-like symptoms with numbness and weakness, relieved by rest and also by sitting down or leaning forward to straighten the lumbar spine. Normal pulses argue against arterial disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with hypertension has sudden, severe, ripping chest pain. His electrocardiogram is normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Computed tomography, chest and abdomen","Reassure him; the tracing is normal","Ankle-brachial index measurement","Abdominal plain radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram may be normal in aortic dissection, so a normal tracing does not exclude it; computed tomography of the chest and abdomen delineates the extent of the dissection. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who smokes and has diabetes reports aching in both buttocks and hips after walking two blocks, relieved by about 10 minutes of rest. He also reports erectile dysfunction. Both femoral pulses are diminished. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tibial artery disease","Aortoiliac occlusive disease","Superficial femoral artery disease","Lumbar spinal stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Buttock and hip claudication points to the aortoiliac segment, and the triad of claudication, absent or diminished femoral pulses and erectile dysfunction is Leriche syndrome, the severe form of aortoiliac disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man presents with painful blue toes and palpable pedal pulses. On abdominal examination there is a pulsatile mass above the umbilicus. Which lesion is the likely source of his emboli?","choices":["Carotid artery plaque","Femoral pseudoaneurysm","Thoracic aortic dissection","Abdominal aortic aneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atheroemboli originate from proximal atherosclerotic plaques, and an abdominal aortic aneurysm can itself shed emboli, producing blue toe syndrome as one of its embolic phenomena. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has a suddenly cool left foot with mild toe pain. She has no sensory loss and no muscle weakness, and venous Doppler signals are present. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Immediate major amputation","Direct transfer to the surgical suite","Discharge with oral aspirin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Viable and marginally threatened limbs, with no sensory loss or weakness, allow sufficient time for vascular imaging such as computed tomography angiography before treatment. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a 5.9 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm is being prepared for repair. Which study is best for diagnosis and pre-intervention planning?","choices":["Abdominal X-ray","Transesophageal echocardiography","Computed tomography angiography","Abdominal ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography is the best diagnostic and pre-intervention planning study, accurately delineating the aneurysm's location, size and extent. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with extensive atherosclerosis develops painful, tender, bluish discoloration of several toes on his left foot, with localized pallor. His dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are easily palpable. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute embolic limb ischemia","Critical limb ischemia","Dry gangrene","Atheroembolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atheroemboli of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris from proximal plaques lodge in small vessels of muscle and skin, causing blue toe syndrome with tenderness and pallor while distal pulses usually stay palpable. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has constant hypogastric and low back pain for two days. A tender pulsatile mass is felt; blood pressure is 152/90 and stable. Imaging shows a 5.8 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm without rupture. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Discharge with smoking advice","Refer once it reaches 6.5 cm","Beta blocker and surgical repair","Ultrasound again in six months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic unruptured aneurysm needs assessment of stability, large-bore intravenous access, pain control, blood pressure control with beta blockers, and surgical repair unless contraindicated. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has constant throbbing, burning pain in her toes and forefoot that is worse lying flat, not relieved by narcotic pain medicine, and eased by dangling her leg or walking around. Her feet are cool. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Peripheral neuropathy","Acute limb ischemia","Critical limb ischemia","Intermittent claudication"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic rest pain of critical limb ischemia is constant, distal, worse with elevation or reclining, poorly controlled even by narcotics, and relieved by dependency or walking, which use gravity to aid perfusion. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with hypertension has a carotid bruit found on routine examination. She has never had neurologic symptoms. Duplex shows 35% stenosis. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Digital subtraction angiography","Medical management","Carotid endarterectomy","Carotid stenting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Medical management is advised for symptomatic and asymptomatic patients with low-grade stenosis under 50%: antiplatelet therapy, control of hypertension, diabetes and lipids, smoking cessation, diet, exercise and weight reduction. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man arrives with a suddenly cold, pale, pulseless left foot. Vascular surgery has been called. Which medical therapy should be started?","choices":["Cilostazol","Pentoxifylline","Intravenous heparin","Oral aspirin alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The medical therapy for acute peripheral arterial occlusion is anticoagulation with intravenous heparin, started alongside an immediate vascular surgery consult. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with a femoral pseudoaneurysm after catheterization reports increasing thigh pain and tingling around the swelling. Which explains her pain?","choices":["Retroperitoneal rupture of the aorta","Critical limb ischemia rest pain","Atheroemboli to the toes","Swelling and nerve compression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm presents with pain from increased pressure due to swelling or from nerve compression by the expanding blood-filled cavity. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man who smokes and has hypertension had an episode of amaurosis fugax (vision in his right eye faded like a curtain descending, then returned after five minutes). A right carotid bruit is heard. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Asymptomatic carotid artery disease","Thoracic aortic aneurysm","Aortic dissection","Symptomatic carotid artery disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Transient monocular blindness from interrupted retinal blood flow is a focal neurologic symptom; with a carotid bruit it classifies him as having symptomatic atherosclerotic carotid artery disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman being treated for an acute aortic dissection develops a new diastolic murmur and worsening breathlessness. Which complication explains these findings?","choices":["Aortic regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Pseudoaneurysm","Carotid stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection can cause aortic regurgitation through dilatation of the aortic root, producing a diastolic murmur and breathlessness. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with claudication and an ankle-brachial index of 0.72 has no bleeding history. Which medication class should be started to lower his risk of myocardial infarction and stroke?","choices":["Intravenous heparin","A loop diuretic","An antiplatelet agent","A thrombolytic agent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antiplatelet therapy (aspirin, clopidogrel or cilostazol) decreases the overall risk of cardiovascular disease from myocardial infarction and stroke and improves walking distance in peripheral artery disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with a known 4.2 cm thoracic aortic aneurysm comes to urgent care with new chest and back pain. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Routine imaging in six months","Reassurance and pain relief","Ankle-brachial index today","Urgent imaging of the aorta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chest or back pain with a known or suspected thoracic aortic aneurysm must undergo urgent imaging to rule out the aneurysm as the cause of the pain. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man who had a large anterior myocardial infarction three weeks ago develops sudden pain and coldness of his right leg with absent foot pulses. Which cardiac finding is the most likely embolic source?","choices":["A calcified mitral leaflet","A pericardial effusion","An atrial septal scar","Left ventricular thrombus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thromboemboli are the most common emboli, and the heart is the source in 80 to 90% of cases; a left ventricular thrombus after myocardial infarction is a named example, as is atrial fibrillation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with carotid artery disease on duplex smokes a pack a day and asks whether quitting still matters now that he takes aspirin. Which counseling point is accurate?","choices":["Smoking harms only the leg arteries","Smoking nearly doubles stroke risk","Aspirin cancels the smoking risk","Quitting only matters before surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation is part of medical management of carotid disease because smoking nearly doubles the risk of stroke; it sits alongside antiplatelet therapy, comorbidity control, diet, exercise and weight reduction. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with claudication, a known contrast allergy and reduced renal function needs arterial imaging before a planned intervention. Which study should be avoided?","choices":["Ankle-brachial index","Computed tomography angiography","Magnetic resonance angiography","Duplex ultrasound"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography uses contrast to show arterial anatomy, and its contraindications are contrast allergy and decreased renal function, both of which she has. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with peripheral artery disease has a hard, dry, black distal toe with a clear line between healthy and dead tissue. There is no swelling, moisture or fever. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atheroembolism","Dry gangrene","Wet gangrene","Gas gangrene"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dry gangrene has a hard, dry texture, usually involves the distal toes, shows clear demarcation between healthy and necrotic tissue, and is common in patients with peripheral artery disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman had a 20-minute episode of right arm weakness and slurred speech that fully resolved. A left carotid bruit is heard. Which is the best initial test for her carotid arteries?","choices":["Digital subtraction angiography","Computed tomography of the chest","Ankle-brachial index","Duplex ultrasonography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Duplex ultrasonography is the most widely used initial screening tool for carotid stenosis and is also used to monitor patients serially for progression or after intervention. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 74"}]};
